Why are some of your antibodies supplied with BSA and sodium azide?
Bovine serum albumin (BSA) is a protein-stabilizing agent, and sodium azide is a preservative that prevents bacterial growth. The carrier protein and preservative are added to increase functionality and longevity of the antibodies. Antibodies supplied with BSA and azide can be stored at 2–4°C.
Do your preparations of HRP-conjugated antibodies contain sodium azide?
HRP is inhibited by sodium azide, so our HRP-conjugated antibodies do not contain this agent. Our unconjugated antibodies are, however, supplied in solutions containing sodium azide.
