m azide is commonly used as a preservative to limit microbial growth. It is hazardous and can affect downstream applications; follow the product safety sheet and assay compatibility guidance. These are general formulation-level interpretations, not proof of the product's stability, biological activity, or suitability for a particular assay.","This data was developed using the same antibody clone in a different buffer formulation containing PBS, BSA, glycerol, and sodium azide ( | staining Myoglobin in Human cardiac muscle tissue sections by Immunohistochemistry (IHC-P - paraformaldehyde-fixed, paraffin-embedded sections). Tissue was fixed with paraformaldehyde; antigen retrieval was by heat mediation in a citrate buffer ( | staining Myoglobin in Rat cardiac muscle tissue sections by Immunohistochemistry (IHC-P - paraformaldehyde-fixed, paraffin-embedded sections). Tissue was fixed with paraformaldehyde; antigen retrieval was by heat mediation in a citrate buffer ( | staining Myoglobin in mouse cardiac muscle tissue sections by Immunohistochemistry (IHC-P - paraformaldehyde-fixed, paraffin-embedded sections). Tissue was fixed with paraformaldehyde; antigen retrieval was by heat mediation in a citrate buffer ( | Related conjugates and formulations (9) | Our carrier-free antibodies are typically supplied in a PBS-only formulation, purified and free of BSA, sodium azide and glycerol. This conjugation-ready format is designed for use with fluorochromes, metal isotopes, oligonucleotides, and enzymes, which makes them ideal for antibody labelling, functional and cell-based assays, flow-based assays (e.g. mass cytometry) and Multiplex Imaging applications. | Storage buffer | pH: 7.2 - 7.4 Constituents: PBS",+4°C,Cold chain indicated,Storage-context temperature at or below 8 °C was extracted (+4°C); verify the exact manufacturer instruction.,Appropriate long-term storage conditions,Properties and storage information | Storage buffer | Appropriate short-term storage condition