19 years of experience",". Insufficiently purified antibodies may retain significant levels of upstream-derived impurities, such as host cell proteins, DNA, viral particles, and media components. These impurities not only compromise the stability and bioactivity of the antibody but may also trigger immunogenicity, allergic reactions, or other safety risks. Additionally, aggregates and degradation products present in the final formulation can interfere with the antibody's mechanism of action (MoA) and diminish its clinical efficacy.
In large-scale commercial manufacturing, suboptimal purification processes may lead to reduced product recovery, compromised process robustness, and even delays in regulatory approval timelines. Therefore, rational design and optimization of antibody purification processes are critical for enhancing product quality, controlling production costs, and ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements.
